# =============================================================================
# WHAT THIS REPOSITORY IS
# =============================================================================
# The top level is deny-by-default: nothing at the root is tracked unless it is
# named below. This is an allow-list rather than a list of exclusions on
# purpose. An exclusion only covers the files somebody predicted, and it has to
# describe them to exclude them; an allow-list covers everything nobody thought
# of, says only what this repository contains, and travels with a clone.
#
# Adding a top-level file or directory to the project means adding one line
# here, in the same commit. That is the intended friction.

/*

!/.github/
!/.gitignore
!/.internal-language.json
!/ARCHITECTURE-PROGRESSIVE.md
!/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
!/CONTRIBUTING.md
!/LICENSE
!/NOTICE
!/PUBLISH-RUNBOOK.md
!/LIMITATIONS.md
!/README.md
!/ROADMAP.md
!/SECURITY.md
!/TRUST.md
!/architecture/
!/benchmark/
!/docs/

!/examples/
!/llms.txt
!/openapi.json
!/openapi.yaml
!/package.json
!/packages/
!/tools/
!/web/

# The deploy copies `llms.txt` and the OpenAPI documents into `web/` so a
# machine finds them at the site root. The repository root is the single source
# of truth for all three; a tracked second copy would be a second thing to
# forget to update, which is the defect check-package-copies.mjs exists to
# prevent elsewhere. Build artefacts, not sources.

/web/llms.txt
/web/openapi.yaml
/web/openapi.json

# `web/guides/` IS TRACKED, and the contrast with the three lines above is
# deliberate rather than an oversight. Those three are copied into place by the
# deploy. The guide pages are not: nothing builds this site, the host serves
# `web/` exactly as it is committed, so a generated page that is not committed
# is a page that does not exist. What makes a tracked generated artefact safe
# here is `npm run check:guides`, which re-renders docs/guides/*.md in memory on
# every commit and fails the build if one byte differs. The thing the ignore
# rules protect against elsewhere -- a second copy that someone forgets to
# update -- cannot survive a commit here.

# The same shape one level down, for the two directories that hold work in
# progress alongside what is published. Same reasoning: name what is here.

/architecture/*
!/architecture/TIER5-DESIGN-NOTE.md
!/architecture/RFC-001-GROUP-SUBJECTS.md

/benchmark/*
!/benchmark/adversarial/
!/benchmark/baseline-convex/
!/benchmark/baseline-raw-s3/
!/benchmark/baseline-supabase/
!/benchmark/baseline-vercel-blob/
!/benchmark/tier1-avatar/
!/benchmark/count-decisions.mjs
!/benchmark/count-loc.mjs
!/benchmark/count-tiers.mjs
# The load benchmark's method and its recorded run are public, because TRUST.md
# links to them and a trust page that cites a file nobody can open is worse than
# one that cites nothing. `results.json` stays out: it is the raw artefact of
# whichever machine ran last, and committing it would make every re-run a diff.
!/benchmark/load/
/benchmark/load/results.json
!/benchmark/decisions.json
!/benchmark/loc.manifest.json

# --- dependencies ------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/

# --- build output ------------------------------------------------------------
dist/
build/
*.tgz

# --- scratch, working directories and tool output ----------------------------
scratch/
outputs/
tmp/
.doccheck/
.measured/
*.log

# `.measured/` holds what `tools/run-suite.mjs` counted, for
# tools/check-version-claims.mjs to compare the published numbers against. It
# is NOT inside `.doccheck/`, which check-doc-samples.mjs removes and recreates
# on every run -- the counts file lived there for about ten minutes and the doc
# checker deleted it, which made the counts look unmeasured rather than wrong.
# Two tools, one scratch directory, one of them with an rm.

# --- local environment -------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# --- editors and operating systems -------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# --- python tooling that has wandered in --------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.pyc
